INT. CHURCH BASEMENT - AA MEETING - NIGHT

Folding chairs in a circle. A coffee urn on a card table. A banner reading ONE DAY AT A TIME hangs crooked over a stack of Christmas decorations. DR. PHILLIPA ROSS, 40s, hospital lanyard still around her neck, sits across from DENNIS CAHILL, 60s, an ex-paramedic in a cardigan too warm for the room.

DENNIS
(raising his paper cup, to the empty chairs)
Hi, I'm Dennis, and I'm an alcoholic.

PHILLIPA
(deadpan, to the empty chairs)
Hi, Dennis.

DENNIS
(leaning in)
Phillipa, we're the only two people here. You can skip the choir response.

PHILLIPA
(beat, sipping)
I like to keep my form.

A door SLAMS upstairs. They both look at the ceiling. Dennis counts to three on his fingers.

DENNIS
(whispering)
That's the bingo group. They're worse than we are.

PHILLIPA
(straightening, clinical)
Dennis. Can I tell you something, as a doctor and not as a drunk?

DENNIS
(settling, hand over heart)
Please. I've been hoping someone would finally diagnose me.

PHILLIPA
(a long breath)
I operated on a man this morning. Thirty-eight. He had a bad liver and a good laugh. He said, Doctor, I'm going to quit. I said, you'd better. And then I came here and sat in a car for forty minutes because I wanted a drink more than I've ever wanted anything.

DENNIS
(softly, no joke)
Did you go in the bar?

PHILLIPA
(shaking her head)
I came here. I came here and I'm telling you I'm scared. She is not supposed to be scared. She is supposed to be the one holding the scalpel.

Dennis looks at her. He does not say it is fine. The urn GURGLES.

DENNIS
(gently)
She's allowed. So is he. So are you.

PHILLIPA
(wiping her eyes, almost laughing)
Dennis. You used three pronouns.

DENNIS
(proud)
I contain multitudes. And a lot of decaf.

The phone on the card table BUZZES. Phillipa glances at it. Her face goes still.

PHILLIPA
(reading, quiet)
It's the hospital. They need me back.

DENNIS
(standing, offering his cardigan, as if it were a medal)
Then go. Come back next week and tell me how it went.

PHILLIPA
(taking the cardigan, absurdly moved)
It smells like a library.

DENNIS
(beat)
It smells like twenty-six months.

She nods. She puts it on over her scrubs. Upstairs, somebody shouts BINGO and the whole ceiling rattles.
